> I did a search on the net for `OpenType' and `pala.ttf'. I got many
> many pages about OpenType fonts, none about pala.ttf and it seems
> that all it is about windows.

Not really.  OpenType fonts can be used on Unix systems also
(e.g. with the FreeType library), but support for TeX resp. Omega is
difficult because the fonts are too intelligent for TeX -- maybe I
find some time to improve my ttf2pk program to support script,
language, and feature tags of OpenType fonts in a generic way
(currently, only vertical glyph representation forms for CJK fonts are
used if necessary).

Since Unicode 3.0, s and t with comma below are encoded in Unicode
(U+0218-U+021B), so there is a good chance that Unicode fonts will
soon have these glyphs also (unfortunately, arialuni.ttf doesn't
contain these glyphs since it has appeared before Unicode 3.0).

> I checked the glyphs in my windows partition on my PC.  Times New
> Roman have the T and t with a comma and S and s with a cedilla.
> Lucida console is with a cedilla for all S, s, T, t. It seems that
> many people make no difference.

Attached are two images which show some glyphs with and without
Romanian glyph representation forms.  The used character codes are
U+015E (S with cedilla) etc.

> Do you know a place where I can download such fonts?

I must admit that I no longer know from where I got pala.ttf -- it is
a commercial font distributed with a MS product (MS office?).

> I am sure that I don't have the required glyphs on my PC.  I
> downloaded a text in unicode and I open it with yudit.  Only 5% of
> the characters are visible, and a long list of warnings that the
> fonts are not found in the specified path.

Within yudit, you must have a single font with all the needed glyphs,
e.g. cyberbit or arialuni.  It is not (yet?)  possible, AFAIK, to have
different fonts for different Unicode subranges.  Note that you can
customize font support in yudit -- many CJK fonts are available for
free download from Microsoft and other Web browser companies.


    Werner

